// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row index_write_through_local_refmut_clobbers_binding (tier 1).
// `q[i] = v` WHERE `q` IS A LOCAL `&mut [T; N]` WRITES INTO `q`'s OWN STORAGE.
//
// THERE IS NO `Drop` IN THIS PROGRAM. The write is simply mis-addressed: the store
// lands on the slot that holds the REFERENCE, not on the element it points at.
// After `q[0] = 77i64` the array is unchanged (`a[0]` still reads 1) and `q` now
// holds the integer 77, so the next read through it dereferences address 77 —
// rc 139, SIGSEGV. This program returns that.
//
// AND THE WRITE IS NOT EVEN CONFINED TO `q`: with a `[i64; 4]` and `q[2] = 77`
// the store goes two words PAST the pointer's slot and the array is still
// unchanged — an out-of-bounds write into the caller's frame, silent, rc 0.
//
// SIX CONTROLS SEPARATE IT, all correct on this binary (lattice block G):
//     (*q)[0] = 77            explicit deref                         -> 77
//     fn f(q: &mut [i64;2])   the same type as a PARAMETER           -> 77
//     a[0] = 77               straight to the local array            -> 77
//     q.a = 77                the same auto-deref sugar at a FIELD   -> 77
//     q.set(0, 77)            a Vec through a local &mut, by method  -> 77
//     n = q[0]                READING through the same binding       -> 1
// So it is exactly: an INDEX WRITE, through the auto-deref sugar, on a LOCAL
// binding of reference type. The parameter control is what rules the TYPE out as
// the discriminator — a parameter's slot already holds the caller's array address,
// so the same GEP lands correctly there by coincidence.
//
// ROOT, by symbol: the `pc == la::INDEX_READ` place branch of `SemaChecker`'s
// place-assignment path (src/compiler/sema_stmt.cpp). `try_index_mut_assign` returns
// nullopt (the receiver's type is a MutRef, not a struct with an `IndexMut` impl),
// the `Index`-without-`IndexMut` refusal below it does not apply either, and the
// fall-through reaches `builder().addr_of_temp(std::move(place), /*is_mut=*/true,
// ...)` — the "real element GEP" the comment describes is taken on the receiver's
// SLOT with no load of the pointer it holds.
//
// ⚠ CORRECTION 2026-09-06 (triage round), measured, supersedes the ROOT and the
// CONTROL reading above on two points:
//   1. THE ROOT IS ONE LAYER DOWN. Sema is correct here: lower_place_assign emits
//      `AddrOfTemp(IndexRead(VarRef q))`, the right shape. The mis-addressing is in
//      MLIRGenImpl::gen_lvalue_addr, `case ec::Code::VarRef`
//      (src/compiler/mlir_gen_expr.cpp): it consults `var_local_ptrs_`, which holds
//      only RAW `*mut`/`*const` locals, and otherwise returns `get_subscript_ptr(vn)`
//      under the comment "or the pointer value (ref/ptr params)" — true of a ref
//      PARAMETER, false of a reference-typed LOCAL, whose slot is an alloca holding
//      the pointer. One missing load. That is also why the parameter control passes.
//   2. "SO IT IS EXACTLY AN INDEX WRITE" IS TOO NARROW. gen_expr_kind(EAddrOfTempView)
//      routes `IndexRead` AND `is_mut && TupleIndex` into that same call, and
//      `q.0 = 77` through a local `&mut (i64,i64)` is clobbered identically — rowed
//      as tuple_index_write_through_local_refmut_clobbers_binding. A `&mut [i64]`
//      SLICE index is CORRECT (SliceIndex loads the descriptor's data pointer in its
//      own branch), and so is a FieldRead. The class is exactly
//      {IndexRead, TupleIndex} x reference-typed LOCAL binding.
//   3. NOT REPRODUCED: the "[i64; 4] ... two words PAST the pointer's slot, an
//      out-of-bounds write into the frame, silent, rc 0" claim above. With two i64
//      canaries declared before the array and `q[0] = 77`, both canaries survive
//      intact (111/222) and the array is merely unchanged. The demonstrated harm is
//      a LOST STORE plus the wild deref this program shows; the frame-corruption
//      claim is unverified and should not be cited until someone reproduces it.
//
// Measured 2026-09-06 on the ownership/drop lattice (g_idxwrite_local_refmut,
// g_idxwrite_struct_elem, g_idxwrite_local_ref_to_array_in_struct, and
// f_assign_index_through_refmut, which loses the store AND leaks the RHS).
// Rust: exit 0.
package index_write_through_local_refmut_clobbers_binding;
extern fn printf(fmt: *const u8, ...) -> i32;
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut a: [i64; 2] = [1i64, 2i64];
    let q: &mut [i64; 2] = &mut a;
    q[0] = 77i64;
    let k: i64 = q[0];
    unsafe { printf("q0=%ld\n".as_ptr(), k); }
    if a[0] != 77i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
